# pedagogy.gbnf
#
# Enforces a strict three-section structure for the Dual-Language
# Pedagogical Engine:
#   1. A fenced Python code block
#   2. An English explanation
#   3. A regional-language explanation, labelled with whatever language
#      name the model is asked to use (Swahili, Amharic, Tigrinya, ...)
#
# What this grammar guarantees: STRUCTURE -- headers, code fence, section
# order, and blank-line spacing are always well-formed, because those are
# hard-coded literals the sampler cannot deviate from.
#
# What this grammar CANNOT guarantee: that the "regional language" section
# is actually written in that language. GBNF constrains the character-level
# shape of the output, not its semantic content -- a model that ignores the
# instruction can satisfy this grammar perfectly while writing English (or
# anything else) inside section 3. That is a model-capability question, not
# something any grammar can enforce. (Verified empirically in this project:
# Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B-Instruct does exactly this for Amharic -- see
# adtc-2026-submission/scripts/test_multilingual_inference.py's results.)
#
# `language-name` is intentionally generic (matches any capitalized word or
# words) rather than hard-coded to one language, so this single static file
# works for any --lang value passed to the engine; getting the model to
# actually write the REQUESTED language into that slot is the system
# prompt's job (src/prompts.py), not this grammar's.

root ::= code-section "\n\n" english-section "\n\n" regional-section

code-section    ::= "### 1. CODE SOLUTION\n```python\n" code-block "```"
english-section ::= "### 2. ENGLISH EXPLANATION\n" prose-block
regional-section ::= "### 3. REGIONAL LANGUAGE EXPLANATION (" language-name ")\n" prose-block

# Any text that never contains a closing triple-backtick fence.
code-block ::= code-char*
code-char  ::= [^\x60] | "\x60" [^\x60] | "\x60\x60" [^\x60]

# Free-form prose. Unbounded on purpose -- GBNF has no negative-lookahead,
# so there is no clean way to force early termination before the next
# section's header without risking rejecting legitimate prose that merely
# contains a "#" character. In practice the model reliably transitions to
# the next required literal once it has said enough, because it is
# instruction-tuned to follow the section template in the system prompt.
prose-block ::= prose-char+
prose-char  ::= [^\x00]

language-name ::= [A-Za-z]+ (" " [A-Za-z]+)*
